%T A395822 16775168,67108864,268427264,1073741824,4294934528,17179869184,
%U A395822 68719345664,274877906944,1099511103488,4398046511104,17592183947264,70368744177664,281474968322048,1125899906842624,4503599593816064
%N A395822 Degree of the minimal polynomial whose root is the square of the sidelength of the largest n-cube that fits inside a unit (n+1)-cube.
%D A395822 Companion to A398182 in the squared variable. If f(n,n+1) is the sidelength of the largest n-cube that fits inside a unit (n+1)-cube, then A398182(n) is the degree of the minimal polynomial of f, while a(n) is the degree of the minimal polynomial in the variable x = f^2. Finding the embedded n-cube has been called the generalized Rupert problem. The Babylonians knew a(1). Nieuwland found a(2) over 100 years after John Wallis solved the original Rupert problem. Kay Pechenick DeVicci Shultz discovered a(3) in the late 20th century. The other values follow from the work of Huber and Ligocki. For n even, a(n) = 2^(n-3)*(2^(n-1)-1) carries the Mersenne factor 2^(n-1)-1 (a(4) = 2*7, a(6) = 8*31, a(8) = 32*127), and 2*a(n) = A398182(n) is an even perfect number for n = 4, 6, 8, 14, 18, 20, 32, ... . For n odd, a(n) = 4^(n-2) is a pure power of 4.
%H A395822 <a href="/index/Rec#order_03">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (4,4,-16).
%F A395822 For n even (n>2), a(n) = 2^(n-3)*(2^(n-1)-1); for n odd (n>1), a(n) = 2^(2*n-4) = 4^(n-2).
%F A395822 G.f.: x*(2*x^2 + 2*x - 1)*(4*x^2 + x - 1)/((1 - 2*x)*(1 + 2*x)*(1 - 4*x)). - _Stefano Spezia_, Aug 04 2026
%e A395822 For n=1, f(1,2)^2 has minimal polynomial x-2 (the unit square's diagonal squared). For n=2, Pieter Nieuwland's value f(2,3)^2 = 9/8 has minimal polynomial 8*x-9. For n=3, Kay Pechenick DeVicci Shultz's octic in f(3,4) becomes (for f(3,4)^2) the minimal polynomial 4*x^4 - 28*x^3 - 7*x^2 + 16*x + 16.
%t A395822 a[0]=0; a[1]=a[2]=1; a[n_]:=If[EvenQ[n], 2^(n-3)*(2^(n-1)-1), 2^(2*n-4)]; Array[a, 30, 0]
%Y A395822 Cf. A398182 (degree in the sidelength itself, equal to 2*a(n)); this is the primary companion. Cf. A093577, A243309, A243313.
%K A395822 nonn,easy
%O A395822 0,4
